# UNLOCK — AI Usage

Canonical AI/product guidance lives in:

* relevant accepted ADRs;
* `docs/PRODUCT.md`;
* `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`;
* current feature-specific design where one exists.

This Cursor rule contains compact AI guardrails.

Do not treat it as a complete AI architecture or generation workflow.

---

## 1. AI Is an Enabling Layer

Prefer, where appropriate:

```text id="ab2h6v"
deterministic logic
→ SQL / statistics
→ cached or precomputed results
→ external deterministic providers
→ AI
```

Use AI when it provides real product value that deterministic approaches do not provide efficiently.

Do not introduce an LLM merely because a task involves text.

---

## 2. AI Is Not the Learning Engine

Do not use an LLM as the authoritative runtime decision-maker for:

* answer correctness;
* mastery;
* misconception state;
* learner-state updates;
* memory scheduling;
* Next Best Action ranking;
* DailyPlan generation or composition.

These behaviors remain deterministic according to accepted Learning Engine policy.

---

## 3. AI Output Is Untrusted Input

Treat model output as untrusted external data.

Where AI output enters application state:

* validate required structure;
* reject malformed output;
* enforce domain constraints;
* do not assume model confidence implies correctness;
* do not bypass normal authorization/data boundaries.

Prefer structured outputs when the feature benefits from them.

Do not trust free-form output directly for persistence-sensitive operations.

---

## 4. Educational Content

When AI generates or transforms learner-facing educational content, preserve the information needed to evaluate its quality.

Depending on the feature, this may include:

* source provenance;
* source references;
* correct-answer support;
* explanation support;
* ambiguity checks;
* content/version identity.

Do not publish generated content merely because generation succeeded.

The exact validation/review workflow belongs to the feature's accepted design.

Do not assume every AI feature requires the same fixed generation pipeline.

---

## 5. Source Grounding

When an AI capability is intended to derive content from supplied learning material:

* preserve the relationship to the source;
* avoid unsupported claims;
* distinguish source-derived content from generated interpretation where relevant.

Do not silently convert missing source support into invented educational facts.

---

## 6. Human / Automated Review

The level of review required for AI-generated content depends on:

* learner risk;
* publication state;
* reversibility;
* source quality;
* content type;
* feature design.

A two-pass generation/verification system may be appropriate for some features.

It is not a universal architectural requirement unless explicitly accepted for that feature.

Do not hard-code a global dual-pass workflow from this rule.

---

## 7. Provider Boundaries

Keep provider-specific details out of core domain/learning logic.

Do not scatter vendor SDK calls through:

* domain logic;
* React components;
* unrelated application use cases.

At the same time, do not create a generalized provider framework merely to prepare for hypothetical future vendors.

Abstract only where current product/runtime needs justify it.

---

## 8. Cost and Latency

AI calls have operational cost and latency.

For AI-enabled features, consider where relevant:

* request frequency;
* token/input size;
* retries;
* duplicate generation;
* caching;
* asynchronous vs interactive expectations;
* provider failure behavior.

Do not put unnecessary AI calls on high-frequency learner paths.

---

## 9. Failure Behavior

AI/provider failure must not silently corrupt trusted application state.

Where relevant:

* validate before persistence;
* fail clearly;
* preserve existing data;
* avoid partial publication;
* avoid presenting malformed content as authoritative.

Do not use fabricated fallback content to hide a failed generation operation.

---

## 10. Privacy and Security

Do not send secrets or unnecessary sensitive data to AI providers.

Minimize provider input to what the feature actually requires.

Respect:

* learner privacy;
* Course/material access;
* authorization;
* source ownership;
* server/client boundaries.

AI integration does not bypass normal UNLOCK security policy.

---

## 11. Feature Flags and Rollout

Use feature flags or staged rollout when they solve a real operational/product need.

Do not require a feature flag for every AI capability automatically.

Follow the existing rollout architecture rather than creating a new flag system solely because AI is involved.

---

## 12. Observability

For material production AI usage, preserve enough observability to understand relevant:

* failures;
* usage;
* cost;
* latency;
* provider behavior.

The exact telemetry design should be proportional to the feature.

Do not build a large AI analytics platform before the product requires one.

---

## 13. Scope Discipline

Do not let an AI feature silently introduce:

* new Learning Engine policy;
* new authorization semantics;
* new publishing rules;
* unsupported source-trust assumptions;
* generalized AI infrastructure;
* provider lock-in or provider abstraction without deliberate design.

If the feature requires a new durable AI/content decision, surface it explicitly.

---

## 14. Core Principle

> Use AI where it creates real product value.

> Keep deterministic learning decisions deterministic.

> Treat model output as untrusted until validated.

> Preserve source provenance where educational correctness depends on it.

> Do not turn one AI feature's workflow into global architecture by accident.
